---
/**
 * /privacy — Privacy Policy.
 *
 * Built Aug 26 2026 from docs/legal/privacy.md, which Fredrick supplied the same day.
 *
 * HELD ON A BRANCH. NOT FOR MERGE UNTIL A CANADIAN PRIVACY PRACTITIONER HAS READ IT.
 * The copy file's own header says "Draft for legal review. Do not publish as written",
 * and that is Fredrick's decision of Aug 26 2026 when the page was planned. `noindex` is
 * set as a second line of defence, not as the hold itself — a page reachable from the
 * footer is public whether or not search engines index it. Remove `noindex` in the same
 * change that clears the review, never before.
 *
 * FOUR DEPARTURES FROM THE APPROVED COPY, all decided by Fredrick on Aug 26 2026 when
 * the page was planned. Nothing else was touched.
 *
 * 1. NO LOCATION LINE. The draft prints "based in Metro Vancouver, British Columbia,
 *    Canada" in the opening and again in section 11. Both are gone under the standing
 *    instruction of Aug 25 2026 that no sentence on any page names a location. Fredrick
 *    was shown that a privacy policy is arguably the exception — PIPEDA wants an
 *    identifiable contact, and a policy that names no jurisdiction is weaker as a legal
 *    document — and chose to apply the rule literally, email only. ONE CONSEQUENCE WORTH
 *    KNOWING: the entity's jurisdiction is now stated nowhere on the site, and a larger
 *    prospect running a vendor assessment may ask for it directly.
 *
 * 2. SECTIONS 2, 4, 5 AND 7 DESCRIBE THE SITE AS IT ACTUALLY IS, AND THEY WERE REWRITTEN
 *    ON AUG 27 2026 WHEN THAT STOPPED BEING "NOTHING". The Aug 26 version said there was
 *    no analytics service, no advertising or tracking pixel, and no cookies of our own.
 *    That was measured and true on the day. The analytics release made every word of it
 *    false in one deploy, which is exactly the failure mode the original note warned
 *    about: the kind of inaccuracy nobody notices, because nothing on the page looks
 *    broken.
 *
 *    WHAT THE PAGE NOW SAYS, and it has to keep matching the build:
 *      - Google Analytics loads on every page for every visitor and CANNOT be switched
 *        off on this site. Stated plainly, with the two things a visitor can actually do
 *        about it, because a policy that describes a control the site does not offer is
 *        worse than one that admits there is none
 *      - Meta Pixel and Microsoft Clarity load only after Accept, and are withdrawable
 *      - LinkedIn and Google Ads are named on the banner and are NOT installed. The page
 *        says so in as many words. The banner names five providers and three of them run;
 *        leaving that unexplained would read as either sloppiness or over-collection
 *      - What Clarity captures, and that typed text is masked and the Cal.com booker sits
 *        in a frame recording cannot see into
 *
 *    RE-MEASURE BEFORE EVERY RELEASE. The rule has not changed, only what it measures.
 *
 * 2b. THE CONTACT FORM, ADDED AUG 28 2026 WITH /contact. A form is a new
 *    personal-information collection point, and shipping one without this page describing
 *    it makes the page untrue — the same failure the analytics release existed to prevent.
 *    THE FORM AND THIS EDIT SHIP IN THE SAME DEPLOY. Three places changed: section 1 gained
 *    the enquiry paragraph, section 5's Netlify row now names form submissions, and section
 *    7's retention line was rewritten (see below).
 *
 *    NO NEW PROVIDER. Netlify Forms was chosen partly for this: submissions are held by the
 *    host already on the table, so the provider list did not grow. Google Forms would have
 *    added a Google iframe setting cookies before consent, and reCAPTCHA would have added a
 *    Google script that had to sit behind the consent gate — at which point the form breaks
 *    for anyone who has not accepted. Neither was built.
 *
 * 2d. THE FORM DESCRIPTION WAS COMPLETED ON SEP 2 2026, and what was wrong with it is worth
 *    knowing: section 1 listed three fields — name, email, message — because it was written
 *    on Aug 28 BEFORE the design build added a fourth. The page understated what the site
 *    collects for five days. **A field list is not prose; it has to be re-read every time the
 *    form changes**, and nothing in the build makes that happen automatically.
 *
 *    Section 2's masking sentence had the same shape of problem: it named only the Cal.com
 *    booker, because when it was written that iframe held the only inputs on the site. It now
 *    names the contact form's fields.
 *
 *    THE MASKING CLAIM IS VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED. Measured on the live site on Sep 2 2026:
 *    distinctive strings were typed into all four fields with real keystrokes, and every
 *    Microsoft Clarity payload from that session was captured at `navigator.sendBeacon` and
 *    gunzipped — 24,745 characters across the whole session, including the submit. The typed
 *    strings appear NOWHERE in it, while `contact-form`, `contact-name` and `contact-message`
 *    all do, and the payload carries runs of bullet characters where the typed text would be.
 *    So Clarity captured the form and substituted the content. The same strings DO appear in
 *    the POST body to arksystems.ca, which is the submission itself and is correct.
 *
 *    RE-RUN THAT TEST IF A FIELD IS EVER ADDED. The standing rule from the analytics release
 *    is that masking is verified before a form ships, and it applies to a changed form too.
 *
 * 2c. SECTION 7'S RETENTION POSITION WAS WEAKENED, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS THE ONE THING ON
 *    THIS PAGE MOST WORTH A SECOND LOOK. It said enquiries were kept for 24 months after
 *    last contact and "then they are deleted". It now says they are kept as business records
 *    with no deletion schedule, with deletion on request.
 *
 *    That was Fredrick's decision of Aug 28 2026, taken after the contradiction was put to
 *    him: the build brief set the new position while this page already published the old
 *    one, and the brief's own wording ("in the same way we keep email correspondence")
 *    pointed at a 24-month rule when read against what the page actually said. The
 *    reasoning for the change is that an enquiry is correspondence a person chose to send
 *    rather than data captured passively, and that a deletion routine nobody performs would
 *    make the page untrue — which is the failure this whole discipline exists to prevent.
 *
 *    WHAT MAKES IT DEFENSIBLE IS THE SENTENCE NEXT TO IT. "You can ask us to delete yours at
 *    any time, and we will" is what keeps an open-ended retention position honest; the right
 *    exists under PIPEDA whether or not the page says so, and stating it costs nothing
 *    operationally because it is only ever exercised on request. IF THAT SENTENCE IS EVER
 *    TRIMMED, the retention position stops being defensible and has to change back.
 *
 *    THIS IS A PUBLISHED COMMITMENT THAT WAS MADE WEAKER. The practitioner review is still
 *    outstanding, and this is the item most likely to come back from it.
 *
 * 3. CAL.COM IS NAMED IN SECTION 2, not only in section 1. BaseLayout's own comment
 *    requires it: embed.js loads from app.cal.com on every page view, before anyone
 *    clicks anything, so it is not merely a booking provider — it receives a request from
 *    every visitor to every page. Section 1 describes what booking sends them; section 2
 *    describes what merely opening a page sends them. Both are true and only one was in
 *    the draft.
 *
 * 4. MICROSOFT CLARITY'S ROW WAS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED, and the check is the point.
 *    Microsoft publishes NO processing region for Clarity: its FAQ asks "Where is my data
 *    stored?" and answers "Your data is stored in the Microsoft Azure cloud service". The
 *    only jurisdiction it names is Microsoft Corporation "(in the United States)", as the
 *    US affiliate that EU customers' data reaches under SCCs. We contract with that entity
 *    directly. The row therefore states the contracting entity's jurisdiction rather than a
 *    data-residency guarantee — see the note in src/lib/legal.ts before repeating it to a
 *    client, because Microsoft could serve Clarity from another Azure region without
 *    contradicting anything it has published. The cross-border paragraph below the table is
 *    what actually protects this, and it is true whichever region it is.
 *
 *    THE RETENTION SENTENCE WAS WRONG AND WAS CORRECTED FROM THE SAME SOURCE. It said only
 *    favourites are kept beyond 30 days. Microsoft's FAQ: "Favorite recordings AND RANDOMLY
 *    SELECTED SAMPLE of recordings are retained for up to 9 months." The nine-month tail is
 *    not something we choose, and copy implying it was would have understated it on the one
 *    page that must not. Heat maps, also nine months, were added at the same time.
 *
 *    SECTION 5 HAS NO PAYMENT PROVIDER ROW, and that is an answer rather than an omission:
 *    there is no third-party payment processor in the path. Invoicing is still described in
 *    section 3, and the records it produces are covered by the Google Workspace row and the
 *    six-year retention rule in section 7. If a processor is ever introduced, this table
 *    needs a row the same day — the copy file's own note is that a missing row is how a
 *    policy goes quietly inaccurate.
 *
 * SECTION NUMBERING IS LOAD-BEARING. The document cross-references itself by number
 * ("the address in section 11", "the table in section 5", and since Aug 27 "see section 2
 * for what stops them"). Sections 2 and 3 lost content to departure 2 and kept their
 * numbers for that reason; section 2 then gained content back on Aug 27 and kept them
 * again. Renumbering means re-reading every cross-reference.
 *
 * ONE THING FREDRICK HAS TO VERIFY BEYOND THE BLANKS: the copy file's build note on
 * section 8 says every safeguard listed must be one that is actually in place, and to
 * delete any line that is not rather than treat it as an aspiration. They ship as
 * written because the copy is approved. An unmet security claim in a privacy policy is a
 * worse exposure than a shorter list.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import LegalDocument from "../components/legal/LegalDocument.astro";
import Pending from "../components/legal/Pending.astro";
import {
  PENDING,
  LEGAL_EFFECTIVE_DATE,
  LEGAL_ENTITY,
  LEGAL_EMAIL,
} from "../lib/legal";

const description =
  "How ArkSystems collects, uses and protects personal information — what we collect, why, who processes it, and how to access or correct it.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={description}
  noindex
>
  <LegalDocument
    title="Privacy Policy"
    dateline={`Effective ${LEGAL_EFFECTIVE_DATE}. Last updated ${LEGAL_EFFECTIVE_DATE}.`}
  >
    {/* The opening is body copy, not a styled standfirst. It carried its own larger size
      * and its own class in the first build; on the plain pattern the document simply
      * starts, which is what the reference does and what every policy page a reader has
      * seen before does. */}
    <p>
      This policy explains what personal information we collect through arksystems.ca and in
      the course of our work, why we collect it, who processes it on our behalf, and how you
      can access or correct it. It applies to this website and to our dealings with clients
      and prospective clients.
    </p>

    <p>
      ArkSystems is operated by {LEGAL_ENTITY}, a company incorporated in{" "}
      <Pending value={PENDING.incorporatedIn} label="jurisdiction of incorporation" />. In
      this policy, &ldquo;we&rdquo; and &ldquo;us&rdquo; mean {LEGAL_ENTITY} operating as
      ArkSystems.
    </p>

    <h2 id="information-you-give-us">1. Information you give us</h2>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">When you email us.</span> Your email address, your name if you
      give it, and whatever you write. We use it to answer you.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">When you book a call.</span> Your name, email address, the time
      you select, and anything you enter in the booking notes. This is collected through
      Cal.com, our booking provider, and is used to hold the appointment and prepare for the
      conversation.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Contact form enquiries.</span> When you write to us through the
      contact form on our <a href="/contact">Contact page</a>, we receive your name, your
      business name if you give it, your email address and your message. We use it to answer
      you and for nothing else. We do not add you to a mailing list, and we do not sell, rent
      or share it. Submissions are received and held by Netlify, which already hosts this
      website. We keep enquiries as part of our business records, in the same way we keep
      email correspondence. You can ask us to delete yours at any time.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">When you complete one of our forms.</span> Our qualification and
      workshop preparation forms ask about your business, your processes, the software you
      use and the people involved. We use that information to determine whether we can help
      you, and to prepare properly if we can. These forms are hosted on Google Workspace.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">When you become a client.</span> In the course of an engagement
      we may be given access to business information, systems and documents, and &mdash;
      depending on the work &mdash; to records that contain personal information about your
      own clients, customers or staff. What we do with that access, and what we keep
      afterwards, is set out on our <a href="/data-handling">Data Handling page</a> and
      governed by our engagement agreement.
    </p>

    <h2 id="collected-automatically">2. Information collected automatically</h2>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Server logs.</span> Our website is hosted by Netlify. Their
      servers record standard technical information about requests, including IP address,
      browser type and the pages requested. This is a normal function of web hosting and is
      used for delivery, security and troubleshooting. It happens on every visit, because
      without it the site cannot be served to you.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Booking.</span> Our booking tool is provided by Cal.com, and the
      script that opens it loads from Cal.com&rsquo;s servers whenever you open a page on
      this site. That means Cal.com receives the same standard technical information
      described above from every page you open here, whether or not you book anything. What
      booking a call sends them is described in section 1.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Analytics.</span> We use Google Analytics to count visits and to
      see which pages are read and which buttons are used. It loads on every page, for every
      visitor, and it is not something you can switch off on this site. It sets cookies that
      give your browser an identifier, so that repeat visits are recognised as the same
      browser. It tells us how the site performs. We do not use it to identify you, and we do
      not send it your name, your email address, or anything you type. If you would rather it
      did not run, your browser&rsquo;s own privacy settings will stop it, as will
      Google&rsquo;s opt-out browser add-on.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Advertising and session recording &mdash; only if you agree.</span>{" "}
      We use the Meta Pixel to measure our advertising, and Microsoft Clarity to record how
      pages are scrolled and clicked so that we can see where the site is confusing. Neither
      of them loads until you press Accept on the cookie notice. If you never answer it,
      neither one runs. You can turn both off again at any time in Cookie settings, and the
      cookies they have already set are deleted when you do.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">What session recording captures.</span> Microsoft Clarity records
      mouse movement, scrolling and clicks. Text typed into input boxes is masked and is never
      recorded. That includes every field on our contact form: your name, your business name,
      your email address and your message are masked as you type them, and the text does not
      reach Microsoft. The booking form is provided by Cal.com inside a separate frame, which
      session recording cannot see into.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Named on the notice, but not running.</span> Our cookie notice
      also names LinkedIn and Google Ads. Neither is installed on this site today. They are
      named because we expect to use them to measure advertising, and we would rather tell you
      before than afterwards. If either one starts running, it will run under the advertising
      setting you have already chosen, and it will be named here as well.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Your fonts and your choice.</span> Our fonts are served from our
      own domain rather than from a font provider. Your answer to the cookie notice is stored
      in your own browser and nowhere else; we keep no record of it on our systems.
    </p>

    <h2 id="why-we-collect-it">3. Why we collect it</h2>

    <p>We collect personal information for these purposes and no others:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>To answer enquiries and correspond with you</li>
      <li>To schedule and prepare for calls and meetings</li>
      <li>
        To assess whether our services suit your business, and to scope, price and deliver
        work
      </li>
      <li>To perform our contract with you, including invoicing and record-keeping</li>
      <li>To meet legal, tax and accounting obligations</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      We do not sell personal information. We do not rent, trade or otherwise disclose it for
      anyone else&rsquo;s marketing purposes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="consent">4. Consent</h2>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">You give consent by providing your information.</span> When you
      email us, book a call or complete a form, you consent to us using that information for
      the purpose it was given.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Cookies.</span> Analytics cookies are set on every visit, and they
      are not covered by the choice we ask you for &mdash; see section 2 for what stops them.
      Advertising and session recording cookies are set only after you press Accept on the
      cookie notice. You can withdraw that at any time in Cookie settings, and the cookies
      already set for those two purposes are deleted when you do.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Commercial email.</span> We may send you commercial electronic
      messages where Canada&rsquo;s Anti-Spam Legislation permits it &mdash; for example,
      where you have asked to hear from us, or where an existing business relationship
      applies. Every such message identifies us, gives our contact information, and includes a
      way to unsubscribe that works. If you unsubscribe, we keep a record of that request so
      we do not contact you again.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">You can withdraw consent at any time</span>, subject to legal and
      contractual restrictions and reasonable notice. Write to us at the address in section
      11. Withdrawing consent may mean we can no longer provide a service.
    </p>

    <h2 id="service-providers">5. Service providers, and where your information is processed</h2>

    <p>
      We use a small number of third-party providers to operate. Each processes information
      only for the purpose we engage it for.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Provider</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
            <th scope="col">Where it processes data</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Netlify</td>
            <td>Website hosting and delivery, and contact form submissions</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Workspace (Google LLC)</td>
            <td>Email, documents, forms, and our prospect records</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cal.com</td>
            <td>Call booking, and the booking script on every page</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google LLC</td>
            <td>Website analytics &mdash; Google Analytics, on every page</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Meta Platforms</td>
            <td>Advertising measurement &mdash; only if you agree</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Microsoft</td>
            <td>Session recording &mdash; Microsoft Clarity, only if you agree</td>
            <td>
              <Pending value={PENDING.clarityRegion} label="Clarity processing region" />
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">
        These providers store and process information outside Canada, principally in the
        United States.
      </span>{" "}
      While personal information is held in another country, it is subject to that
      country&rsquo;s laws and may be accessible to that country&rsquo;s courts, law
      enforcement and national security authorities under those laws. We use providers we
      consider reputable and require them to protect information to a comparable standard, but
      we cannot exclude that access.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">How long session recordings are kept.</span> Microsoft Clarity
      keeps a recording for 30 days. Two kinds are kept for up to nine months: recordings we
      mark as favourites, and a sample of recordings that Microsoft selects at random. Heat
      maps, which show where visitors clicked and how far they scrolled, are also kept for up
      to nine months.
    </p>

    <p>
      If your business cannot accept processing outside Canada, tell us before an engagement
      begins. It affects which tools we can use to deliver the work, and it is a normal thing
      to require.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-do-not-do">6. What we do not do</h2>

    <p>We do not sell, rent or trade personal information.</p>

    <p>
      We do not add you to a marketing list because you emailed us, booked a call, or
      downloaded something.
    </p>

    <p>
      We do not use your business information, your documents, or anything we see during an
      engagement to train AI models.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where a build uses third-party AI providers, we name them &mdash; and the tier &mdash;
      in writing before anything is connected, together with what their terms say about how
      inputs are handled. Their terms are theirs, and they change; what we commit to is that
      you see them before you decide, not afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-long-we-keep-it">7. How long we keep it</h2>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Enquiries that don&rsquo;t become work.</span> Emails, contact
      form submissions, booking records and prospect notes are kept as part of our business
      records, in the same way we keep email correspondence. We don&rsquo;t run a deletion
      schedule for them. You can ask us to delete yours at any time, and we will.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Client records.</span> Contracts, statements of work, proposals,
      requirements documents, workshop documents and invoices are kept for the length of the
      relationship and for six years after the end of the tax year they relate to, because
      Canadian tax law requires business records to be retained for that period.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Your operational data &mdash; we don&rsquo;t keep it.</span>{" "}
      Anything given to us to examine, test with or build against is working material, not a
      record. It is deleted within 90 days of the engagement closing. We do not retain client
      operational data after an implementation. Nothing of yours sits on our systems once the
      work is done.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Website analytics.</span> Google Analytics keeps the visit records
      it collects for 14 months. Session recordings are covered in section 5.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Unsubscribe records</span> are kept indefinitely, because that is
      what makes an unsubscribe permanent.
    </p>

    <h2 id="safeguards">8. Safeguards</h2>

    <p>
      We protect personal information with measures appropriate to how sensitive it is: access
      limited to those who need it, multi-factor authentication on accounts that hold client
      information, encryption in transit, and removal of access when an engagement ends.
    </p>

    <p>
      No system is perfectly secure, and we do not claim otherwise. If a breach occurs that
      creates a real risk of significant harm, we will notify affected individuals and the
      Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada as the law requires.
    </p>

    <h2 id="your-rights">9. Your rights</h2>

    <p>
      You can ask us what personal information we hold about you, ask for a copy, and ask us to
      correct anything inaccurate. Write to the address in section 11.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will respond within 30 days. If we need longer, we will tell you why and when to
      expect a response. If we cannot give you access, we will explain the reason.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you are not satisfied with how we have handled your information, you may complain to
      the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada at{" "}
      <a href="https://priv.gc.ca" rel="noopener">priv.gc.ca</a>, or to the Office of the
      Information and Privacy Commissioner for British Columbia at{" "}
      <a href="https://oipc.bc.ca" rel="noopener">oipc.bc.ca</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="children">10. Children</h2>

    <p>
      This website is intended for businesses. We do not knowingly collect personal
      information from anyone under the age of majority.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">11. Contact</h2>

    <p>Questions about this policy, or requests for access or correction:</p>

    <div class="doc-contact">
      <p>
        <span class="doc-term">
          Fredrick <Pending value={PENDING.privacyOfficerSurname} label="surname" />, Privacy
          Officer
        </span>
      </p>
      <p>ArkSystems, operated by {LEGAL_ENTITY}</p>
      {/* Plain text, not a mailto: link. CLAUDE.md records that no mailto: remains
        * anywhere on the site and that it must not be reintroduced. A displayed,
        * monitored address is still an accessible contact route, which is what PIPEDA
        * asks for — the requirement is that someone can reach you, not that a click
        * opens a mail client. */}
      <p>{LEGAL_EMAIL}</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      We do not publish a mailing address. A monitored email address is the contact route, and
      we will supply anything further on request.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">12. Changes to this policy</h2>

    <p>
      We update this policy when what we do changes. The effective date at the top shows when
      it last changed. Material changes affecting existing clients will be communicated
      directly rather than by quietly editing this page.
    </p>
  </LegalDocument>
</BaseLayout>
